<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jules Brunet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1838">1838</a>–<a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jules Brunet was a French army officer sent with a military mission to train the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s troops in 1867. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> broke out he left the French army and joined the shogunate loyalists, serving the Republic of Ezo as an adviser until its defeat in 1869. He later rose to general and chief of staff to the French war minister, and his story partly inspired the film The Last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">Samurai</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jules-brunet.jpg" alt="Jules Brunet" width="320" height="582" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JulesBrunetAlone.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jules Brunet was born on 2 January 1838 in Belfort, in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the son of Jean-Michel Brunet, an army veterinary doctor. He entered Saint-Cyr in 1855, moved to the École Polytechnique, joined the horse artillery and graduated fourth in his course from the artillery school at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> in 1861. In the French invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> he served with distinction, above all at the Siege of Puebla in 1863, for which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> awarded him the Légion d'honneur, and his quick sketches of the war were published in French newspapers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At 28 Brunet was one of the youngest officers of the mission of fifteen, led by Captain Charles Chanoine, that reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in January 1867. When the mission was ordered home in 1868, he resigned from the French army on 4 October, writing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> that the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyos</a> had asked him to be the soul of their resistance. With André Cazeneuve and Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enomoto-takeaki">Enomoto Takeaki</a> he went north and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakodate">Hakodate</a> on 26 October 1868, and in the Republic of Ezo Brunet acted as foreign minister and second-in-command of the army under Otori Keisuke. Its 3,000 men were defeated by 7,000 imperial troops in June 1869.</p><p>Evacuated by the French corvette Coëtlogon, Brunet was arrested at Yokohama and sent back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which refused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s request to punish him. After a six-month suspension he rejoined the army in February 1870, fought at Spicheren, Mars-la-Tour and Gravelotte, was captured at the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> and then served in the suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a>. He commanded the 11th Artillery Regiment from 1887 to 1891, became a brigade general in December 1891, and in 1898 Chanoine, then minister of war, made him his chef de cabinet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Brunet retired into the reserve in January 1903 and died in Fontenay-sous-Bois after a long illness. Through the influence of Enomoto, by then navy minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> forgave him and awarded him medals in 1881 and 1885, among them the Order of the Rising Sun.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jules Brunet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the shogunate surrendered Edo in 1868, Admiral Enomoto Takeaki sailed north with much of the shogunal navy, picking up defeated soldiers and French military advisers including <b>Jules Brunet</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-ezo">Republic of Ezo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Belfort, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Fontenay-sous-Bois, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.64,6.86&amp;year=1885" aria-label="Belfort on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jules Brunet"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5395 6029h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5395 6029h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.64,6.86&amp;year=1885">What was here in 1885? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
